A bid is only valid if it clears the current highest bid by at least the minimum increment — checked and applied atomically, not read-then-compared-then-written:

```java
BidResult placeBid(String auctionId, String bidderId, BigDecimal amount) {
    return jdbcTemplate.execute(conn -> {
        Auction auction = lockAuctionForUpdate(conn, auctionId);
        BigDecimal minValid = auction.getCurrentHighBid().add(auction.getMinIncrement());
        if (amount.compareTo(minValid) < 0) {
            return BidResult.rejected(minValid);
        }
        recordBid(conn, auctionId, bidderId, amount);
        auction.setCurrentHighBid(amount);
        maybeExtendClosingWindow(conn, auction);
        return BidResult.accepted(amount);
    });
}
```

Locking the auction row for the duration of the check-and-write is what makes "clears the current high bid by the minimum increment" an atomic decision rather than a race.

A `ProxyBid` reacts to a new competing bid by raising the bidder's own bid to the minimum needed to stay ahead — never revealing the stored maximum unless forced to bid it:

```java
void onNewHighBid(Auction auction, Bid newBid) {
    for (ProxyBid proxy : auction.getActiveProxyBids()) {
        if (proxy.getBidderId().equals(newBid.getBidderId())) continue;
        BigDecimal needed = newBid.getAmount().add(auction.getMinIncrement());
        if (proxy.getMaxAmount().compareTo(needed) >= 0) {
            BigDecimal counterBid = needed.min(proxy.getMaxAmount());
            placeBid(auction.getId(), proxy.getBidderId(), counterBid);
        }
    }
}
```

The counter-bid is `min(needed, maxAmount)` — just enough to retake the lead, capped at the stored max — so competitors only ever see the smallest amount required to beat them, never the ceiling the bidder was actually willing to pay.
